---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import {
  CAMPAIGN_PARAMS,
  CAMPAIGN_VALUE_MAX,
  CONSENT_STORAGE_KEY,
  GA_PROPERTY_ID,
  MEASUREMENT_ID
} from '../lib/analytics-url.js';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
const analyticsCookie = `_ga_${MEASUREMENT_ID.slice(2)}`;
---
<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy | ClearCalc Tools"
  description="Privacy policy for ClearCalc Tools. Calculator inputs stay in the browser. Google Analytics 4 runs only after you accept and does not receive calculator values."
>
  <section class="legal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p>Last updated: November 12, 2026.</p>
    <p>This policy describes how ClearCalc Tools (clearcalctools.com) handles information when you use the site. The site is operated by Zayed, an individual, who can be reached at <a href="mailto:zayed70010@gmail.com">zayed70010@gmail.com</a>. ClearCalc does not require an account.</p>
    <h2>Calculator inputs</h2>
    <p>Each calculator runs in your browser. When you press Calculate, the page applies the formula on that page to the values in the form and shows the result on the same page. Those values are not sent to a ClearCalc server, stored in an account, or written into a ClearCalc database. The pages do not save calculator inputs in browser storage. You need JavaScript for a result to appear. ClearCalc does not receive a copy of the numbers you type. They are not added to Google Analytics, to a page-view hit, or to any form event. A result on the page is not a message to Google.</p>
    <h2>What the site collects</h2>
    <p>The site is hosted on Cloudflare. Like other hosts, Cloudflare can process technical connection data needed to deliver and protect the site, such as your IP address, the browser type you send, the URL you request, and the time of the request. Those hosting logs are separate from Google Analytics. Cloudflare's own privacy notice is at <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">cloudflare.com/privacypolicy</a>. Those logs may be processed in the United States or in other countries where the host operates.</p>
    <p>If you email <a href="mailto:zayed70010@gmail.com">zayed70010@gmail.com</a>, the message contains your email address and whatever you chose to write, which may include calculator inputs you paste in. That message is used to reply and to look into an error, a suggestion, or a partnership note. It is not sold.</p>
    <h2>Google Analytics</h2>
    <p>ClearCalc can measure visits with Google Analytics 4, measurement ID <code>{MEASUREMENT_ID}</code>, property <code>{GA_PROPERTY_ID}</code>. The tag is the Google tag for that measurement ID. Its script is loaded from Google's tag host. There is no Google Tag Manager container. There is no advertising tag and no ads.txt file.</p>
    <p>The same question is shown to every visitor. The site does not look up a country or region to decide whether to ask. Until you accept, the page does not load the Google tag, does not send a measurement request, and does not set an analytics cookie. Rejecting leaves measurement off. There is no cookieless measurement ping before an acceptance.</p>
    <p>The purpose is to understand which pages are opened, whether a visit stays long enough for Google Analytics to treat it as engaged, and which websites or campaign links referred the visit. It is not used to advertise, to build an ad profile, or to read calculator inputs. Google describes how Analytics handles data from sites that use it at <a href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/6004245" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Safeguarding your data</a>. Google's privacy policy is at <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">policies.google.com/privacy</a>.</p>
    <h3>What a hit can contain</h3>
    <p>After you accept, the page sends one page view for that page load. The address in the hit keeps the path, such as <code>/calculators/bmi-calculator/</code>. The fragment, the part after <code>#</code>, is removed. The query string is removed except for a fixed list of campaign parameters. A value is kept only when it is at most {CAMPAIGN_VALUE_MAX} characters and contains only letters, numbers, and the characters <code>.</code> <code>_</code> <code>~</code> and <code>-</code>. The parameters are:</p>
    <ul>
      {CAMPAIGN_PARAMS.map((name) => <li><code>{name}</code></li>)}
    </ul>
    <p><code>utm_term</code> is not on that list, because it is meant to carry a search phrase. Advertising click ids are not kept either, including <code>gclid</code>, <code>dclid</code>, <code>gbraid</code>, <code>wbraid</code>, and <code>fbclid</code>. A calculator field in the query string, such as a price, a wage, a weight, or a height, is removed. Changing the address later to add a result does not send another page view, and a later engagement hit uses the same reduced address.</p>
    <p>The referring address, when the browser shares one, is reduced to that site's origin and path. Its query string and fragment are removed, so a search query does not ride along. The page title can be included. The title is the title of the page, not the numbers you typed.</p>
    <p>Google's tag also sends the technical fields it uses for a page view, such as a client identifier, the browser language, and timing. The page tells the tag to set Google signals and ads personalization to false, and to redact ads data. In the Google Analytics property, enhanced measurement is limited to page views, form interactions and site search are off, and Google signals, user-provided data, granular location and device data, and ads personalization are off. The page does not send a user id, an email address, or a form event. It blocks form, scroll, click, file-download, and site-search events if the tag tries to send them. It does allow Google's later <code>user_engagement</code> hit, which is how engaged sessions are measured, after the same address reduction.</p>
    <p>The connection that carries a hit reaches Google, so the connection includes an IP address. Google's privacy controls say that in Google Analytics, IP addresses are not logged or stored (<a href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/9019185" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Privacy controls in Google Analytics</a>). A separate page describes the handling for people in the EU, Switzerland, and the UK (<a href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/12017362" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU, Switzerland, or UK-focused data and privacy</a>). ClearCalc does not receive the hit on its own server and does not operate Google's logging.</p>
    <p>A page view counts a browser that accepted analytics and ran the tag. It is not a count of every person who opened the site. People who reject analytics, block the script, or use a browser that never runs it are absent. Google filters some automated traffic. That filter does not prove that every remaining hit came from a person, or that every automated visit was removed.</p>
    <h3>The choice, and how to change it</h3>
    <p>The first visit shows a banner with <strong>Reject analytics</strong> and <strong>Accept analytics</strong>. The choice is stored in this browser under the local storage key <code>{CONSENT_STORAGE_KEY}</code>. That entry is not a cookie and is not sent to Google. It stays until you change it or clear site data for this site. The footer control <strong>Analytics choices</strong> opens the same banner, so you can accept later or withdraw an acceptance. Withdrawal stops later hits from this browser, tells the tag that analytics storage is denied, and deletes the Analytics cookies the page can see. A hit that has already reached Google cannot be pulled back from Google by this page. You can also use <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google's Analytics opt-out browser add-on</a>, which sets its own block. A privacy request can go to <a href="mailto:zayed70010@gmail.com">zayed70010@gmail.com</a>.</p>
    <h2>Cookies</h2>
    <p>ClearCalc's own pages do not set a cookie to remember an account. The calculators do not need a cookie to run. The accept or reject choice is stored in local storage, not in a cookie.</p>
    <p>If you accept analytics, Google's tag sets its own first-party cookies. Google's cookie page describes <code>_ga</code>, used to distinguish browsers, and <code>{analyticsCookie}</code>, used to keep session state. Google's default lifetime for those cookies is two years. This page does not set a different <code>cookie_expires</code> value. A cookie override in the property's tag settings would apply instead; this policy does not state such an override, because none was part of the configuration described here. Browsers can delete the cookies sooner. Google notes a maximum of 400 days in Chrome and 7 days in Safari when the visitor does not return (<a href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/11397207" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cookie usage on websites</a>). The page asks the browser to mark the cookies <code>SameSite=Lax</code> and <code>Secure</code>.</p>
    <p>Google's cookie page also says that linking a property to a Google Ads account can make the tag set more cookies. This site does not load an advertising tag. The page sets ads personalization and Google signals to false and blocks advertising hosts such as DoubleClick. This policy does not describe a Google Ads link, because that was not part of the configuration confirmed for this measurement.</p>
    <p>The host may set a cookie that is strictly necessary to deliver or secure the connection. You can block or delete cookies in your browser. Blocking cookies does not change the calculator math. Blocking every cookie after an acceptance can stop Google Analytics from recognizing the browser on a later visit.</p>
    <h2>Advertising cookies</h2>
    <p>ClearCalc does not place an advertising script on these pages, and it does not have an ads.txt file. No advertisement is served from this site's code. Google Analytics on this site is not an advertising tag.</p>
    <p>The rest of this section is the rule that would apply to advertising cookies, including any Google advertising cookies, if an advertising script were added later. None is installed today. Third-party vendors, including Google, use cookies to serve ads based on a person's prior visits to a website and to other websites when an advertising tag is present. Google's use of advertising cookies, on a site that uses them, enables it and its partners to serve ads based on visits to that site and other sites.</p>
    <p>You may opt out of personalized advertising by visiting <a href="https://adssettings.google.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google's Ads Settings</a>. How Google uses advertising cookies is explained at <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/ads" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">How Google uses advertising cookies</a>. You can opt out of some other third-party vendors' use of cookies for personalized advertising at <a href="https://www.aboutads.info" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">aboutads.info</a>.</p>
    <h2>Selling and sharing</h2>
    <p>ClearCalc does not sell personal information. It does not share personal information for cross-context behavioral advertising. No advertising script is installed, and ads personalization and Google signals are off. If you accept analytics, the page measurement described above is sent to Google. That disclosure is not a sale. If advertising cookies are added later, that use can count as a "share" under California law. You can use the opt-out links above, and you can email <a href="mailto:zayed70010@gmail.com">zayed70010@gmail.com</a> with the subject "Do not sell or share my personal information". That request will be honored.</p>
    <h2>How long information is kept</h2>
    <p>Calculator inputs are not kept by ClearCalc at all. The analytics choice stays in this browser until you change it or clear site data. How long Google keeps user-level and event-level Analytics data is a setting on the property, described at <a href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/7667196" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Data retention</a>. Google's help says a standard property can be set to 2 months or 14 months, and that a large property can be limited to 2 months. This page does not set that control and does not state which period applies to property {GA_PROPERTY_ID}. Hosting logs are kept by the host for its own security and reliability periods. Email you send is kept long enough to answer you and to fix or consider the point you raised, and then deleted when it is no longer needed for that, unless a longer period is required by law.</p>
    <h2>United Kingdom and European rights</h2>
    <p>If you are in the United Kingdom or the European Economic Area, you can ask what personal information is held about you, ask for a copy, ask for a correction, or ask for it to be deleted. You can object to processing based on legitimate interests, ask for a restriction, and, where the law gives it, ask for information you provided to be sent to you or to someone else. You can also complain to a supervisory authority. In the UK that is the Information Commissioner's Office, at <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint</a>.</p>
    <p>Analytics cookies and the Analytics tag are used only after you accept. The site asks every visitor that question, not only visitors in the UK or the EEA. You can withdraw that acceptance with <strong>Analytics choices</strong>. Withdrawal stops later hits from this browser. Hosting logs still rely on legitimate interests, so the pages stay available and secure. The message you send is the basis for an email you choose to write. Calculator inputs are handled on your device and are not a record ClearCalc collects. To use any of these rights, email <a href="mailto:zayed70010@gmail.com">zayed70010@gmail.com</a>. There is no separate data-protection officer.</p>
    <h2>California rights</h2>
    <p>If you are a California resident, you can ask ClearCalc to tell you the categories and specific pieces of personal information collected about you, the sources, the purpose, and the categories of third parties it is disclosed to. You can ask for deletion. You can ask ClearCalc not to sell or share your personal information. You will not be treated differently for asking. The categories this site can hold are identifiers in an email you send (your email address and the contents of the message), internet activity the host records in a connection log (such as IP address and the page requested), and, if you accept analytics, an online identifier in the Analytics cookies and internet activity about the pages opened and the referring site, disclosed to Google for the measurement described above. ClearCalc does not sell that information and does not share it for cross-context behavioral advertising.</p>
    <p>Send a request to <a href="mailto:zayed70010@gmail.com">zayed70010@gmail.com</a>. Say that you are making a California privacy request, and say whether you want to know, delete, or opt out of sale or sharing. An authorized agent may email for you if you say so in the message and the agent provides enough for Zayed to confirm the request is yours. The same address works if you live somewhere else and want to make a similar request.</p>
    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>ClearCalc is written for adults doing everyday calculations. It is not directed at children under 13, and it does not knowingly collect personal information from them. Analytics stays off unless the visitor accepts. The site does not check a visitor's age before showing that question. If you believe a child has emailed personal information, write to <a href="mailto:zayed70010@gmail.com">zayed70010@gmail.com</a> and ask for it to be deleted.</p>
    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>If this policy changes, the "Last updated" date at the top of this page will change with it. A material change to advertising or analytics will be described here before that service is turned on. The Google Analytics section above is that description for this measurement. The tag still waits for an acceptance.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Privacy questions and requests go to <a href="mailto:zayed70010@gmail.com">zayed70010@gmail.com</a>. You can also start from the <a href={`${base}contact/`}>contact page</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Layout>
